| $-2$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, ${f}_{X}(x)={f}_{Y}(y)$ for corresponding values of $X$ and $Y.$ Therefore, the distribution of $Y$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nSo, we have $a=1,b=-2,c=\\frac{1}{4},$ and therefore $a+b+c=-\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398081 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398082 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398081 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398083 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398081 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398084 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398081 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398085 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398081 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398086 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398081 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398081 1077 "selection" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398081 1079 #edb/ref 17592186398082 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398081 1080 #uuid "e4194eca-59dd-5baf-88db-f9e5ba650594" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398082 1081 "-\\frac{3}{4}" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398082 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398082 1084 #uuid "056c1bc3-87b1-52ff-9899-6ace1944f684" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398083 1081 "\\frac{13}{4}" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398083 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398083 1084 #uuid "02c61e00-6a3e-5e56-9137-e5dccb1de07e" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398084 1081 "-\\frac{5}{8}" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398084 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398084 1084 #uuid "acd15a23-334a-5351-8f6c-a0c66a649f39" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398085 1081 "-\\frac{7}{8}" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398085 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398085 1084 #uuid "bd911c24-2a46-5378-8b6a-c99505a8bdf8" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398086 1081 "\\frac{13}{8}" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398086 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398086 1084 #uuid "c0336929-9562-5bf5-a8b3-18039b85f7a6" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398088 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398087 1069 "q-111789" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398087 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398087 1071 "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398087 1072 #uuid "96dc0ffa-c035-5c4a-b42f-7a4928dfb8df" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398087 1074 "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the point $(2,-1),$ which is defined by the (repeated) columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{-1-0}{2-0}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x.$" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398088 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398089 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398088 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398090 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398088 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398091 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398088 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398092 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398088 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398093 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398088 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398088 1077 "selection" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398088 1079 #edb/ref 17592186398090 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398088 1080 #uuid "39de4ad4-8ca2-5e4e-a502-b05807674875" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398089 1081 "{R}^{2}" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398089 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398089 1084 #uuid "dda9e3d7-e6ee-5825-845d-f01fdb4e4b75" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398090 1081 "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398090 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398090 1084 #uuid "cdba0602-2fd0-52ae-8f15-118e4a6f97e5" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398091 1081 "(0,0)" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398091 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398091 1084 #uuid "f2332fcb-d82d-5111-a699-5798822a8e76" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398092 1081 "y=2x" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398092 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398092 1084 #uuid "3cf49b0b-ca8d-5df2-944e-152ab812be35" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398093 1081 "y=-2x" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398093 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398093 1084 #uuid "75145a95-95a2-5300-a3b1-4c2ffbe7d0e2" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398095 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398094 1069 "q-112815" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398094 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398094 1071 "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=7{x}^{3}+42{x}^{2}$ have multiplicity $2?$" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398094 1072 #uuid "c78b7fa0-e6a0-5d03-8538-f39afab9a911" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398094 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $7{x}^{3}$ and $42{x}^{2}$ is $7{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =7{x}^{3}+42{x}^{2} \\\\  & =7{x}^{2}(x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $x$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x+6)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-6$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398095 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398096 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398095 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398097 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398095 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398098 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398095 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398099 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398095 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398100 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398095 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398095 1077 "selection" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398095 1079 #edb/ref 17592186398097 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398095 1080 #uuid "eeb8e003-ac5d-547c-aeff-048096576722" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398096 1081 "x=-6" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398096 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398096 1084 #uuid "b123c8c7-4f1d-5408-975f-32f6bc27b94c" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398097 1081 "x=0" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398097 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398097 1084 #uuid "4fbed8c3-42d9-5e77-8dee-f6c715eb878b" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398098 1081 "x=0,6" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398098 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398098 1084 #uuid "30eda07a-1025-5c98-a57d-c93c05dbf778" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398099 1081 "x=7" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398099 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398099 1084 #uuid "0673668c-baa9-5ad3-9ebd-b6d66539f06e" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398100 1081 "x=-6,0" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398100 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398100 1084 #uuid "8827287c-1b1d-53c9-bec1-38ed29947161" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398102 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398101 1069 "q-112968" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398101 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398101 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x)=8{x}^{6}-7{x}^{2}+x-6,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398101 1072 #uuid "584a8f77-ca02-5f77-bbb3-b9ad8836332c" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398101 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-6,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 3$ and $\\pm 6.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $8,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,$ $\\pm 2,$ $\\pm 4$ and $\\pm 8.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to the lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{8}, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that several potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{8},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{4},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398103 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398104 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398105 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398106 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398102 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398107 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398102 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398102 1077 "selection" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398102 1079 #edb/ref 17592186398103 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398102 1080 #uuid "a41a2631-5f05-5cb0-8884-5d26ad87d7dc" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398103 1081 "20" 13194139533473 17592186045585 true] [17592186398103 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533473 1759218